</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Evaluation</span></h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Evaluation Metrics</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the capabilities of LLMs in generating parallel code, we adopt a validation methodology inspired by prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>, leveraging <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">HeCBench</span>’s support for the automated compilation and execution with output verification. Specifically, we evaluate model outputs on the held-out test split of the <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ParaTrans</span> dataset. This framework enables assessment of both the <em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">compilation rate</em> – the percentage of generated programs that compile successfully – and the <em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">validation rate</em>, the percentage of generated programs producing outputs that match the expected output specified in the <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">HeCBench</span> kernel. For validation, we replaced the <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">main</span> function in the generated code with the <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">main</span> function in the ground-truth kernel, as the <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">main</span> function in the ground-truth kernel contained the output verification check.
In places where the code compiled successfully before the replacement but failed after the replacement, we used 3 attempts with GPT-4o-mini to repair the compilation error. We then verified that no changes were made that affect kernel functionality.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Experimental Setup</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluated the performance of two instruction-tuned foundation models: GPT-4o-mini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite> (referred to as GPT in short in the paper) and LLaMA-3.3-70B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> (referred to as LLaMA in short in the paper). These models are selected for their accessibility and strong performance on a wide range of general-purpose code generation tasks, without requiring additional fine-tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM Hyperparameters.</span> LLMs include several hyperparameters that significantly affect their outputs. The main ones are <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">temperature</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">top-p</span>, and <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">max tokens</span>. <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Temperature</span> adjusts the randomness (aka “creativity”): higher values lead to more diverse outputs, while lower values make responses more deterministic. <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Top-p</span> (nucleus sampling) sets a cumulative probability threshold, retaining only the most likely tokens whose total probability exceeds the cutoff. <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Max tokens</span> limits the number of tokens the model may generate in its response (excluding the prompt). After empirical tuning (see <a href="#S5.SS1" title="V-A RQ1: Translation Behavior with Zero-shot Prompting ‣ V Results and Analysis ‣ UniPar: A Unified LLM-Based Framework for Parallel and Accelerated Code Translation in HPC" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">subsection V-A</span></a>), we found that a <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">temperature</span> of 0.2 and a <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">top-p</span> of 0.9 provided reliable results across both models. We used a maximum token limit of 15,000 whenever possible to accommodate the widest range of model outputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM Quantization.</span> To fine-tune the model, we used 16-bit floating point precision, which offers a good balance between computational efficiency and training stability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>. For inference, we applied 8-bit quantization to reduce memory usage and improve latency without significantly impacting model performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hardware.</span> For LLaMA model inference, we used four NVIDIA L40 GPUs and twelve Intel Xeon Gold 6336Y CPUs. GPT-4o-mini inference was performed via the Azure OpenAI API <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>. For the verification evaluation, OpenMP code was executed on a machine equipped with fifty AMD EPYC 7742 CPUs, and CUDA code was run on the same NVIDIA L40 GPUs mentioned above. Fine-tuning was conducted using eight NVIDIA A100 GPUs (80GB).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Results and Analysis</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first answer RQ1, exploring different temperatures
to present the baseline performance of the chosen LLMs in the zero-shot (<a href="#S5.SS1" title="V-A RQ1: Translation Behavior with Zero-shot Prompting ‣ V Results and Analysis ‣ UniPar: A Unified LLM-Based Framework for Parallel and Accelerated Code Translation in HPC" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">subsection V-A</span></a>). Next, we examine the few-shot prompt settings (<a href="#S5.SS2" title="V-B RQ1: Translation Behavior with Few-shot Prompting ‣ V Results and Analysis ‣ UniPar: A Unified LLM-Based Framework for Parallel and Accelerated Code Translation in HPC" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">subsection V-B</span></a>). We then evaluate the effectiveness of supervised fine-tuning (<a href="#S5.SS3" title="V-C RQ2: Supervised Fine-Tuning ‣ V Results and Analysis ‣ UniPar: A Unified LLM-Based Framework for Parallel and Accelerated Code Translation in HPC" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">subsection V-C</span></a>) and the agentic compilation repair pipeline (<a href="#S5.SS4" title="V-D RQ3: Agentic Translation with Compiler Feedback ‣ V Results and Analysis ‣ UniPar: A Unified LLM-Based Framework for Parallel and Accelerated Code Translation in HPC" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">subsection V-D</span></a>) on the baseline performance of the models.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">V-A</span> </span><span id="S5.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1</span><span id="S5.SS1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">: Translation Behavior with Zero-shot Prompting</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluated both LLaMA-3.3-70B-Instruct and GPT-4o-mini across a range of decoding configurations. Specifically, we tested three <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">temperature</span> values (0.2, 0.6, and 0.9) and three <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">max token</span> limits (5,000, 10,000, and 15,000), while keeping the <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">top-p</span> value fixed at 0.8 in a zero-shot prompting setup. Empirically, varying <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">top-p</span> did not produce significant improvements in performance, and with a very slight improvement when switching from 0.8 to 0.9. was therefore held constant.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in  <a href="#S5.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ V-A RQ1: Translation Behavior with Zero-shot Prompting ‣ V Results and Analysis ‣ UniPar: A Unified LLM-Based Framework for Parallel and Accelerated Code Translation in HPC" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 2</span></a>, lower <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">temperature</span> settings yielded both higher and more consistent results across models, with the largest effect observed in the configurations allowing longer outputs. This suggests that reduced randomness in the token selection process improves syntactic validity, especially when generating larger kernel codes, as supported by the findings in previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>. This finding is particularly important when accommodating a broader variety of kernels, such as more complex or larger kernels. The rest of our experiments were all done with a temperature of 0.2, top-p of 0.9, and max output tokens set to 15000.</p>
</div>
